            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "pc_info reports how many points a cloud holds, where it reaches, and where its middle is.\n\nIt changes nothing - the cloud passes straight through from 'cloud out' - so it can sit anywhere in a chain while you look.\n\nIT IS FOR SETTING THE OTHERS UP:\nPut it after the sensor while you are choosing crop bounds: the min and max it reports ARE the numbers to type into pc_crop, and you shrink in from there. Put it after a filter and watch the count while you tune - it is how you catch a filter eating the subject.\n\nAN EMPTY CLOUD:\nWhen a cloud arrives with no points in it, pc_info sends 0 from 'count' and nothing from min, max or centroid, but still passes the empty frame on from 'cloud out' - so a renderer or voxel node after it clears when the room empties.\n\nSYNTAX:\npc_info\n\nEXAMPLE:\npc_info\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\npoint cloud:\nThe cloud, as an (N, 3) array or a cloud frame. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncount:\nHow many points.\n\nmin / max:\nThe smallest and largest x, y and z found - the corners of the box that just holds the cloud.\n\ncentroid:\nThe average position of all the points.\n\ncloud out:\nThe cloud, unchanged - frame, metadata and all.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 790,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 560,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "pc_info reports how many points a cloud holds, where it reaches, and where its middle is.\n\nIt changes nothing - the cloud passes straight through from 'cloud out' - so it can sit anywhere in a chain while you look.\n\nIT IS FOR SETTING THE OTHERS UP:\nPut it after the sensor while you are choosing crop bounds: the min and max it reports ARE the numbers to type into pc_crop, and you shrink in from there. Put it after a filter and watch the count while you tune - it is how you catch a filter eating the subject.\n\nAN EMPTY CLOUD:\nWhen a cloud arrives with no points in it, pc_info sends 0 from 'count' and nothing from min, max or centroid, but still passes the empty frame on from 'cloud out' - so a renderer or voxel node after it clears when the room empties.\n\nSYNTAX:\npc_info\n\nEXAMPLE:\npc_info\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\npoint cloud:\nThe cloud, as an (N, 3) array or a cloud frame. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncount:\nHow many points.\n\nmin / max:\nThe smallest and largest x, y and z found - the corners of the box that just holds the cloud.\n\ncentroid:\nThe average position of all the points.\n\ncloud out:\nThe cloud, unchanged - frame, metadata and all."
        },
